      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the equation \\(7^{x+7} = 8^x\\), we will take the natural logarithm of both sides of the equation. However, it's easier to work with logarithms of base 8 first, so let's proceed by setting \\(\\log_8(y) = x\\).\n\nFirst, rewrite the equation by taking the logarithm of both sides:\n\n\\[\n\\log_8(7^{x+7}) = \\log_8(8^x)\n\\]\n\nUsing the logarithmic property \\(\\log_b(b^y) = y\\), we simplify the right side:\n\n\\[\n(x + 7) \\log_8(7) = x\n\\]\n\nNow, solve for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\nx \\log_8(7) + 7 \\log_8(7) = x\n\\]\n\nRearrange terms to isolate \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\n7 \\log_8(7) = x - x \\log_8(7)\n\\]\n\nFactor out \\(x\\) on the right-hand side:\n\n\\[\n7 \\log_8(7) = x(1 - \\log_8(7))\n\\]\n\nOur goal is to solve for \\(x\\), so divide both sides by \\(1 - \\log_8(7)\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{7 \\log_8(7)}{1 - \\log_8(7)}\n\\]\n\nWe are given \\(x\\) in the form \\(\\log_8(7^7)\\). To express \\(x\\) this way, we rewrite \\(\\log_8(7^7)\\) using the change of base formula \\(\\log_a(b) = \\frac{\\log_c(b)}{\\log_c(a)}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\log_8(7^7) = \\frac{\\log_{8^7}(7^7)}{\\log_{8^7}(8^7)} = \\frac{\\log_8(7) \\cdot 7}{1} = \\frac{7 \\log_8(7)}{1} = 7 \\log_8(7) = x\n\\]\n\nSo, we have:\n\n\\[\nx = \\log_8(7^7)\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(b\\) is \\(8\\). Finally, we find the answer:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{8}\n\\]",
